corsica sits here a short distance from  the italian coast throughout the  medieval and early modern periods its  people spoke corsican and italian it was  economically culturally and politically  tied to the italian peninsula for most  of its existence and yet today it's a  part of france which raises the question  why why is corsica french and not  italian so throughout the medieval  period corsica swapped hands a few times  from pisa to genoa to aragon to france  to milan to a bank briefly to france  again and finally back to genoa and  across these years the people of corsica  had revolted numerous times and retaking  the islands often proved costly for its  overlords and in that same spirit came  the year 1729 when a heavy tax burden  led the people of corsica to revolt  against their genoese rulers it briefly  became a kingdom before declaring the  creation of a republic previously  revolts in corsica were nasty but  ultimately doomed affairs however this  time the revolt lasted for decades  because now the corsicans were being  supported by others and by others i mean  the british this wasn't out of some  kind-hearted wish to see the corsicans  prosper that would be silly in fact the  actual reason was to deprive the french  of the islands as the genoese were  paying french troops to suppress the  rebellion as the rebellion went on the  genoese knew that they'd lost the island  and so opted to sell it to france france  agreed to the sale and soon after sent a  lot of troops to corsica and the  rebellion was crushed in 1769 so that's  france's control over corsica  established and settled then right well  no when the french revolutionary wars  broke out corsica rebelled again and  soon threw in the lot with the british  as a sort of shared kind of sovereign  british colony the french retook the  island after a couple of years and they  were in possession of it when napoleon  was defeated and exiled he briefly came  back was defeated again and as a part of  the congress of vienna europe's major  powers decided to take france's recent  gains from her yet corsica remained a  part of france and this was mostly due  to france buying it prior to the french  revolution since the goal of the  congress was to mostly reset europe to  before the french revolution not to  simply humiliate france which upset the  corsican since all that british support  didn't go very far as such corsica would  remain a part of france for the  foreseeable future but its inclusion  wasn't guaranteed you see up until the  mid 19th century italian was the most  spoken language on the island and so to  many in what would soon become italy  corsica was one of their own one man  with such an opinion was giuseppe  garibaldi who after the conquest of rome  by italy in 1870 called for the  immediate invasion of corsica to which  the italian king said no because he  didn't feel like picking a fight with  france when world war 1 kicked off there  were some discussions by the central  powers that if italy were to join their  side corsica as well as some other  territories would be handed to them if  they won of course italy sided with the  anton but the idea of corsica rightfully  belonging to italy would persist until  benito mussolini came to power as you  know italy joined world war ii by  invading france and after its full italy  didn't get corsica it remained a part of  vichy france until 1942 when germany  decided that it didn't need to exist  anymore and so along with italy they put  an end to it with this italy came into  control of corsica which mussolini had  wanted and this control lasted a whole  year since italy was defeated in 1943.  soon afterwards the brand new italy had  joined the allies and from it french  troops liberated the island and  thereafter corsica would remain a part  of france i hope you enjoyed this  episode and thank you for watching with  a special thanks to my patrons james  bissonette kelly money maker corso wolfe  jordan longl